  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY NIGHT THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 252 PM MDT Thu Sep 3 2026\n\nLong-term guidance is currently suggesting that the tri-state area \nshould continue to see effects from a fairly strong longwave ridge, \nslowly propagating across the plains and upper-midwest Monday. This \nsetup will keep our area in southwest flow aloft through most of the \nforecast period. Model guidance is keying in on an upper-level \njet streak, which should begin to eject thru this ridge on \nTuesday. Deamplification of said ridge is expected through the \nend of the four day timeframe, which should make the region more\nsusceptible to shortwave passages. At the surface, low-pressure\nsets up over western Kansas through next Thursday. A slight \ncool down, accompanied by daily chances for afternoon/evening \nshowers and thunderstorms is anticipated over the course of the \nweek due to lessened effects of the ridge.\n\nEnsemble guidance is hinting at daily PoPs (Probability of \nPrecipitation) through Thursday of next week as shortwaves move \nthrough the synoptic system. There is also medium confidence of a \nfrontal boundary setting up and moving through the county warning \narea (CWA) late Tuesday into Wednesday. For Monday, PoPs\ncurrently stand at 30-65% for locations north of I-70 with \nchances south of the interstate being much lower. A more uniform\n15-35% is forecasted for the area Tuesday. Wednesday and \nThursday should follow suit with a bit more modest 15-30% PoPs. \nDewpoints are forecasted in the mid 50s to 60s, providing ample \nmoisture to the environment. Confidence of thunderstorms forming\nin the CWA is low at this point in time due to lower \ninstability values forecasted across the region.\n\nUnseasonably hot and breezy conditions will persist throughout the \nregion as the ridge evolves. High temperatures in the low to upper \n90s Monday transition to more upper 80s/low 90s by Thursday. Low \ntemperatures should follow a similar pattern, going from 60s/70s to \nmore uniform 60s across the CWA. Surface winds are forecasted to \nstart off Monday at 15-20 mph, gusting up to 30 mph. The stronger \nwinds will occur on the more northern and western areas of the tri-\nstate region, 15-30 mph are expected there along with gusts up to 40 \nmph. Winds should weaken for the remainder of the forecast period, \nas 5-15 mph is anticipated, with gusts up to 25 mph.",
